<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-make-orthog">
<title>Solving for orthogonality</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	determine all values of <m> k </m> for which <m> \uvec{v} </m> is
	<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthogonal" text="custom">orthogonal</xref></term>
	to <m> \uvec{w} </m>.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{v} = (3,k,4) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{W} = (-1,-3,6) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Calculate <md> \udotprod{v}{w} = 3 \cdot (-1) + k \cdot (-3) + 4 \cdot 6 = 21 - 3 k </md>.
		Two vectors are orthogonal precisely when their dot product is <m> 0 </m>,
		so set the above expression equal to <m> 0 </m> and solve for <m> k </m>:
		<md> 21 - 3 k = 0 \qquad \implies \qquad k = 7 </md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{v} = (k^2, 3, -6 k, 4) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{W} = (1,-3,-2,5) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Calculate <md> \udotprod{v}{w} = k^2 \cdot 1 + 3 \cdot (-3) + (-6 k) \cdot (-2) + 4 \cdot 5 = k^2 + 12 k + 11 </md>.
		Two vectors are orthogonal precisely when their dot product is <m> 0 </m>,
		so we would like to know when the above expression is equal to <m> 0 </m>.
		Factoring,
		we have <md> k^2 + 12 k + 11 = (k + 1) (k + 11) </md>,
		so the two vectors are orthogonal precisely when either <m> k = -1 </m> or <m> k = -11 </m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
